# agents/permissions.allow — the permission rules every machine's agents get.
#
# Claude Code's rule syntax (the superset), one rule per line, full-line # comments,
# first wins on a duplicate. `dots` (install.sh → `t permissions --apply`) merges the
# list into ~/.claude/settings.json verbatim, and translates every Bash PREFIX rule
# (`Bash(<words>:*)`) into codex's ~/.codex/rules/dotfiles.rules and cursor's
# ~/.cursor/cli-config.json; everything else (WebFetch, mcp__, Skill, WebSearch, an
# exact `Bash(cmd)`) is claude-only. `t permissions --show` prints the translations.
#
# ENV ASSIGNMENTS — `Bash(*=* node *)`: Claude strips a leading `NAME=value` only for
# its built-in safe list (NODE_ENV, CI, TZ …), so `DATABASE_URL=… node …` needs a rule
# that spells the assignment out (`*=*` = any variable, any value; SPACE spelling only —
# the `:*` form of such a rule is accepted by Claude and never matches, and the lint
# refuses it). Cursor gets it as a whole-line glob. Codex CANNOT take it: its parser
# hands a script with an assignment (or a redirect) to the rules as one opaque token —
# and the prompt such a script raises is the sandbox blocking the network, so the same
# `dots` turns the codex workspace-write sandbox's network on (add-only,
# ~/.codex/config.toml `[sandbox_workspace_write] network_access = true`; set it false
# by hand to keep the sandbox offline — a hand-set value is never flipped).
#
# ADD-AND-RETIRE: deleting a rule here leaves it on every machine that already has it
# (the merge never removes what it does not know). To take a rule back, MOVE it to
# permissions.retire. `mcp__sessions` is not here: install_claude_mcp_allow owns it.
#
# Harvested 2026-09-14 from the ff / cfp project settings.local.json files, the
# global settings, codex's default.rules and cursor's cli-config.json — minus the
# one-offs (`ps -p 55669`, a literal curl PUT), the machine paths (a
# ~/Documents/Development tree that no longer exists), AND the project-specific rows
# (a project's MCP tool names, its skills, the WebFetch domains it researched). This
# repo is PUBLIC: it carries commands and generic tool rules only — no home paths, no
# hosts, nothing that says what you work on. Pinned by test_shipped_lists_are_well_formed.

